    "title": "GAUCHE definition in American English | Collins English Dictionary",
    "text": "COBUILD frequency band gauche (goʊʃ ) adjective If you describe someone as gauche, you mean that they are awkward and uncomfortable in the company of other people. We're all a bit gauche when we're young. Collins COBUILD Advanced Learner’s Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers You may also like COBUILD frequency band gauche in American English (ɡoʊʃ ) adjectiveOrigin: Fr, fig. meaning (lit., on the left) < MFr gauchir, to become crooked, warped, ult. < Frank *wankjan, to totter (akin to Ger wanken), confused with *walken, to beat, full (cloth) Webster’s New World College Dictionary, 5th Digital Edition. Copyright © 2025 HarperCollins Publishers. Derived forms gauchely (ˈgauchely) adverb gaucheness (ˈgaucheness) noun COBUILD frequency band gauche in American English (ɡouʃ) adjective lacking social grace, sensitivity, or acuteness; awkward; crude; tactless Their exquisite manners always make me feel gauche SYNONYMS inept, clumsy, maladroit; coarse, gross, uncouth.Most material © 2005, 1997, 1991 by Penguin Random House LLC. Modified entries © 2019 by Penguin Random House LLC and HarperCollins Publishers Ltd Derived formsgauchelyadverb gauchenessnoun Word origin [1745–55; ‹ F: awkward, left; MF, deriv. of gauchir to turn, veer ‹ Gmc] COBUILD frequency band gauche in British English (ɡəʊʃ ) adjectivelacking ease of manner; tactless Collins English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers Derived formsgauchely (ˈgauchely)adverb gaucheness (ˈgaucheness)noun Word originC18: French: awkward, left, from Old French gauchir to swerve, ultimately of Germanic origin; related to Old High German wankōn to stagger",
